\section{Advanced Term Rewriting}
\label{sec:advanced-term-rewriting}

\subsection{Reduction Strategies}
\label{sec:reduction-strategies}

When a term has multiple redexes (subterms matching the left-hand side of a rule), the choice of which redex to reduce first defines a \emph{reduction strategy}:

\begin{definition}[Reduction Strategies]
\label{def:reduction-strategies}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Normal order} (outermost-leftmost): Reduce the outermost redex first. This strategy always finds a normal form if one exists.
  \item \textbf{Applicative order} (innermost-leftmost): Reduce the innermost redex first (evaluate arguments before calling). This may diverge even when a normal form exists.
  \item \textbf{Lazy evaluation}: Like normal order, but shares common subexpressions to avoid redundant computation.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Normal Order is Normalizing]
\label{thm:normal-order}
If a term $t$ has a normal form (a term with no redexes), then normal-order reduction will find it. This is the \emph{standardization theorem} for term rewriting.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Applicative Order Divergence]
\label{ex:applicative-diverge}
Consider the rules:
\begin{align*}
  \texttt{first}(x, y) &\to x \\
  \texttt{loop} &\to \texttt{loop}
\end{align*}
The term $\texttt{first}(0, \texttt{loop})$ has normal form $0$. Normal-order reduction finds it:
\[
\texttt{first}(0, \texttt{loop}) \to 0
\]
But applicative-order reduction diverges, attempting to reduce $\texttt{loop}$ first:
\[
\texttt{first}(0, \texttt{loop}) \to \texttt{first}(0, \texttt{loop}) \to \cdots
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Completion: The Knuth--Bendix Algorithm in Detail}
\label{sec:knuth-bendix-detail}

The Knuth--Bendix completion algorithm transforms a set of equations into a convergent (confluent and terminating) rewrite system, if one exists.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Complete a set of equations $E$ into a rewrite system $R$}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $R \gets \emptyset$
\For{each equation $l = r$ in $E$}
  \State Orient into a rule: if $l > r$ add $l \to r$; if $r > l$ add $r \to l$; else fail
\EndFor
\Repeat
  \State Find all \textbf{critical pairs} $(s_1, s_2)$ from overlapping rules in $R$
  \For{each critical pair $(s_1, s_2)$}
    \State Compute normal forms: $s_1' = \text{NF}(s_1)$, $s_2' = \text{NF}(s_2)$
    \If{$s_1' \neq s_2'$}
      \State Orient $s_1' = s_2'$ into a new rule and add to $R$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\Until{no new critical pairs}
\State \textbf{return} $R$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{definition}[Critical Pair]
\label{def:critical-pair}
Given two rules $l_1 \to r_1$ and $l_2 \to r_2$ (with disjoint variables), a \textbf{critical pair} arises when a non-variable subterm of $l_1$ unifies with $l_2$. If $l_1|_p$ unifies with $l_2$ via substitution $\sigma$ (at non-root position $p$), the critical pair is $(\sigma(r_1), \sigma(l_1)[\sigma(r_2)]_p)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Completion of Group Theory]
\label{ex:completion-groups}
The equational theory of groups:
\begin{align*}
  x \cdot (y \cdot z) &= (x \cdot y) \cdot z \quad &\text{(associativity)} \\
  e \cdot x &= x \quad &\text{(left identity)} \\
  x^{-1} \cdot x &= e \quad &\text{(left inverse)}
\end{align*}
Using the recursive path ordering with $^{-1} > \cdot > e$, completion derives additional rules:
\begin{align*}
  x \cdot e &\to x \quad &\text{(right identity)} \\
  x \cdot x^{-1} &\to e \quad &\text{(right inverse)} \\
  (x^{-1})^{-1} &\to x \quad &\text{(double inverse)}
\end{align*}
The completed system is convergent and provides a decision procedure for the equational theory of groups.
\end{example}

\subsection{Termination Orderings}
\label{sec:termination-orderings}

To prove termination of a TRS, one must find a \emph{well-founded ordering} $>$ such that $l > r$ for every rule $l \to r$, and $>$ is closed under substitution and context.

\begin{definition}[Reduction Ordering]
\label{def:reduction-ordering}
A \textbf{reduction ordering} is a well-founded strict order $>$ on terms satisfying:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Monotonicity}: $s > t$ implies $f(\ldots, s, \ldots) > f(\ldots, t, \ldots)$
  \item \textbf{Substitution}: $s > t$ implies $s\sigma > t\sigma$ for all substitutions $\sigma$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Common reduction orderings include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Lexicographic path ordering} (LPO): Compares terms lexicographically based on a precedence on function symbols.
  \item \textbf{Recursive path ordering} (RPO): Extends LPO with multiset comparison of arguments.
  \item \textbf{Knuth--Bendix ordering} (KBO): Uses a weight function on terms combined with a precedence.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Termination via Ordering]
\label{thm:termination-ordering}
If there exists a reduction ordering $>$ such that $l > r$ for all rules $l \to r$ in a TRS, then the TRS is terminating.
\end{theorem}

\section{Initial Algebras: Deeper Treatment}
\label{sec:initial-algebras-deep}

\subsection{Free Algebras and Term Algebras}
\label{sec:free-algebras}

The initial algebra is closely related to the concept of a \emph{free algebra}---an algebra freely generated by a set of elements.

\begin{definition}[Free Algebra]
\label{def:free-algebra}
Given a signature $\Sigma$ and a set $X$ of generators, the \textbf{free $\Sigma$-algebra} $F_\Sigma(X)$ is the $\Sigma$-algebra such that for every $\Sigma$-algebra $A$ and every function $f : X \to A$, there exists a unique $\Sigma$-homomorphism $\hat{f} : F_\Sigma(X) \to A$ extending $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Term Algebra is Free]
\label{thm:term-free}
The term algebra $T_\Sigma(X)$ (the algebra of all terms with variables from $X$) is the free $\Sigma$-algebra on $X$.
\end{theorem}

The initial algebra $T_{\Sigma, E}$ is the quotient of the free algebra $T_\Sigma(\emptyset)$ (ground terms) by the congruence generated by the equations $E$.

\subsection{Initial Algebra Semantics for Programming Languages}
\label{sec:initial-alg-pl}

Initial algebra semantics can be used to give meaning to entire programming languages. The key idea is to define the syntax as a signature and the semantics as equations:

\begin{example}[Initial Algebra Semantics for While]
\label{ex:while-initial}
The While language can be given initial algebra semantics by defining:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Signature}: Sorts $\texttt{Com}$ (commands), $\texttt{State}$ (states), $\texttt{Val}$ (values); operations for assignment, sequencing, conditionals, and loops.
  \item \textbf{Equations}: The standard semantic equations (e.g., $\texttt{seq}(\texttt{skip}, c) = c$, $\texttt{if}(\texttt{true}, c_1, c_2) = c_1$).
\end{itemize}
The initial algebra of this specification is the ``standard'' semantics of While, where commands are interpreted as state transformations.
\end{example}

\subsection{Parameterized Specifications}
\label{sec:parameterized-specs}

Real-world specifications often need to be \emph{parameterized} over other specifications. For example, a specification of stacks parameterized over the element type:

\begin{definition}[Parameterized Specification]
\label{def:parameterized-spec}
A \textbf{parameterized specification} is a mapping from specifications to specifications. For example:
\[
\texttt{Stack}[E : \texttt{Elem}] = \text{the specification of stacks of elements from } E
\]
Instantiating with $E = \texttt{Nat}$ yields $\texttt{Stack}[\texttt{Nat}]$, the specification of stacks of natural numbers.
\end{definition}

In categorical terms, a parameterized specification is a \emph{functor} from the category of specifications to itself. The initial algebra semantics extends to parameterized specifications via the theory of \emph{initial algebra semantics for parameterized specifications} (Goguen and Meseguer, 1985).

\section{Equational Logic and Proof}
\label{sec:equational-logic}

\subsection{Birkhoff's Deduction System}
\label{sec:birkhoff-deduction}

Birkhoff (1935) gave a complete proof system for equational logic:

\begin{definition}[Equational Deduction]
\label{def:equational-deduction}
From a set of equations $E$, the following rules derive new equations:
\begin{align*}
  \textsc{Reflexivity:} &\quad \vdash t = t \\
  \textsc{Symmetry:} &\quad \frac{\vdash s = t}{\vdash t = s} \\
  \textsc{Transitivity:} &\quad \frac{\vdash s = t \quad \vdash t = u}{\vdash s = u} \\
  \textsc{Substitution:} &\quad \frac{\vdash s = t}{\vdash s\sigma = t\sigma} \\
  \textsc{Congruence:} &\quad \frac{\vdash s_1 = t_1 \quad \cdots \quad \vdash s_n = t_n}{\vdash f(s_1, \ldots, s_n) = f(t_1, \ldots, t_n)}
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness of Equational Logic]
\label{thm:equational-completeness}
Birkhoff's deduction system is \textbf{complete}: if $E \models s = t$ (i.e., $s = t$ holds in all models of $E$), then $E \vdash s = t$ (i.e., $s = t$ is derivable from $E$ using the rules above).
\end{theorem}

This is the \emph{Birkhoff completeness theorem}, the equational analogue of the completeness theorem for first-order logic.

\subsection{Rewriting as Proof}
\label{sec:rewriting-as-proof}

When a TRS is convergent (confluent and terminating), rewriting provides a \emph{decision procedure} for the equational theory:

\begin{theorem}[Rewriting Decision Procedure]
\label{thm:rewriting-decision}
If $R$ is a convergent TRS, then $E_R \models s = t$ if and only if $s$ and $t$ have the same normal form with respect to $R$.
\end{theorem}

This reduces the problem of checking equational validity to the mechanical process of computing normal forms---a task that can be automated.

\begin{example}[Proof by Rewriting]
\label{ex:proof-rewriting}
To prove $\texttt{add}(\texttt{succ}(0), \texttt{succ}(0)) = \texttt{succ}(\texttt{succ}(0))$ using the TRS of Example~\ref{ex:nat-trs}:
\begin{align*}
  \texttt{add}(\texttt{succ}(0), \texttt{succ}(0)) &\to \texttt{succ}(\texttt{add}(0, \texttt{succ}(0))) \\
  &\to \texttt{succ}(\texttt{succ}(0))
\end{align*}
Since $\texttt{succ}(\texttt{succ}(0))$ is already in normal form, both sides reduce to the same term, proving the equation.
\end{example}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Checking an Equation by Replaying Contextual Rewrites}

A checker can trust the stated equations without trusting the
heuristic that found a proof. Use finite first-order term trees
over a fixed signature. The input consists of source and target
terms and a finite list of certificate steps. Each step names an
equation, a direction, a path of child indices, and a simultaneous
substitution assigning a well-formed term to every variable of that
equation. Direction determines which side must match at the chosen
position. The checker uses syntactic equality; it does not try to
find a more convenient instance or infer an omitted substitution.

\begin{sudocode}[Replay a proof as equal-term replacements]
\Function{CheckEquation}{$s,t,E,C$}
  \State $u\gets s$
  \ForAll{steps $(i,d,p,\theta)$ in $C$ in order}
    \State Read oriented equation $E[i]$ in direction $d$ as $(l,r)$
    \State Require a valid path $p$ and substitution $\theta$
    \State $v\gets$ subtree of $u$ at $p$
    \If{$v\ne l\theta$}
      \State \Return rejected \Comment{Match the full instance}
    \EndIf
    \State Replace subtree at $p$ by $r\theta$
    \Comment{Preserve the surrounding context}
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $(u=t)$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Take equations $\mathrm{add}(x,0)=x$ and
$\mathrm{add}(0,x)=x$. The source is
$\mathrm{add}(\mathrm{add}(0,a),0)$ and the target is $a$.
First choose the left child, use the second equation forward,
and substitute $a$ for $x$. The focused subtree matches
$\mathrm{add}(0,a)$ and the replacement leaves
$\mathrm{add}(a,0)$. Next choose the empty path to the root and
use the first equation with the same substitution. The new term
is $a$ and final equality succeeds. Using the first equation
at the left child instead would demand the syntactically
different subtree $\mathrm{add}(a,0)$.

\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
The current term equals the source in every model of the given
equations: substitution justifies the instance and congruence
justifies replacement inside the unchanged context. An empty
certificate proves only syntactic reflexivity. Replay terminates
even when the equations cannot generate a convergent rewrite
system, since it follows a finite supplied list. Rejecting
one certificate does not show that the equation is false.

\section{Inductive Data Types and Structural Recursion}
\label{sec:inductive-types}

\subsection{Structural Recursion}
\label{sec:structural-recursion}

Functions on inductive data types are defined by \emph{structural recursion}---pattern matching on the constructors and recursing on the sub-components.

\begin{definition}[Structural Recursion]
\label{def:structural-recursion}
Given an inductive type $T$ with constructors $c_1, \ldots, c_n$, a function $f : T \to A$ defined by structural recursion has one clause per constructor:
\begin{align*}
  f(c_1(x_1, \ldots, x_k)) &= e_1[f(x_{i_1}), \ldots, f(x_{i_m})] \\
  &\vdots \\
  f(c_n(x_1, \ldots, x_k)) &= e_n[f(x_{j_1}), \ldots, f(x_{j_p})]
\end{align*}
where the recursive calls $f(x_{i_j})$ are on \emph{strict sub-components} of the argument.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Termination of Structural Recursion]
\label{thm:structural-termination}
Every function defined by structural recursion on a well-founded inductive type terminates.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Fold and Catamorphisms}
\label{sec:fold-catamorphisms}

The universal pattern of structural recursion is captured by the \emph{fold} operation (also called a \emph{catamorphism} in category theory):

\begin{definition}[Fold/Catamorphism]
\label{def:fold}
Given a functor $F$ and its initial algebra $(\mu F, \text{in})$, the \textbf{catamorphism} $\banana{f} : \mu F \to A$ for an $F$-algebra $f : F(A) \to A$ is the unique homomorphism from $(\mu F, \text{in})$ to $(A, f)$. It satisfies:
\[
\banana{f} \circ \text{in} = f \circ F(\banana{f})
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Fold on Lists]
\label{ex:fold-lists}
For lists, the functor is $F(X) = 1 + A \times X$ and the initial algebra is $(\texttt{List}, [\texttt{nil}, \texttt{cons}])$. A fold on lists:
\[
\banana{(c, f)}\; \texttt{nil} = c \qquad \banana{(c, f)}\; (\texttt{cons}(x, xs)) = f(x, \banana{(c, f)}\; xs)
\]
This is the familiar \texttt{foldr} from functional programming.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Fusion Law for Folds]
\label{thm:fusion}
If $h \circ f = g \circ F(h)$, then $h \circ \banana{f} = \banana{g}$. This \emph{fusion law} allows optimizing compositions of folds by fusing them into a single pass.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Unfold and Anamorphisms}
\label{sec:unfold-anamorphisms}

The dual of fold is \emph{unfold} (anamorphism), which \emph{generates} data from a seed value:

\begin{definition}[Unfold/Anamorphism]
\label{def:unfold}
Given a functor $F$ and its final coalgebra $(\nu F, \text{out})$, the \textbf{anamorphism} $\ana{f} : A \to \nu F$ for an $F$-coalgebra $f : A \to F(A)$ is the unique homomorphism from $(A, f)$ to $(\nu F, \text{out})$. It satisfies:
\[
\text{out} \circ \ana{f} = F(\ana{f}) \circ f
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Unfold for Streams]
\label{ex:unfold-streams}
An infinite stream of natural numbers starting from $n$, incrementing by 1:
\[
\texttt{from}(n) = \ana{f}(n) \quad \text{where } f(n) = (n, n+1)
\]
The anamorphism generates the stream $n, n+1, n+2, \ldots$
\end{example}
